  "guidebook": {
    "intro": "Swahili counts in a neat, regular way, and it tells the time from sunrise. Once you know that, a bus at saa mbili will never catch you out.",
    "phrases": [
      { "target": "Ni saa ngapi sasa?", "base": "What time is it now?" },
      { "target": "Saa moja asubuhi.", "base": "Seven in the morning (the first hour of daylight)." },
      { "target": "Saa tatu na nusu.", "base": "Half past nine." },
      { "target": "Saa sita mchana.", "base": "Twelve noon." },
      { "target": "Shilingi elfu moja.", "base": "One thousand shillings." },
      { "target": "Tutaonana kesho.", "base": "See you tomorrow." },
      { "target": "Pole pole, tafadhali.", "base": "Slowly, please." }
    ],
    "tips": [
      {
        "id": "tens",
        "title": "Tens and teens",
        "body": [
          "The teens are “ten and…”: **kumi na moja** (11), **kumi na mbili** (12). The tens from 20 to 90 come from Arabic: **ishirini**, **thelathini**, **arobaini**, **hamsini**, **sitini**, **sabini**, **themanini**, **tisini**.",
          "Put them together with **na**: **ishirini na tano** (25). Hundreds and thousands come first: **mia tano** (500), **elfu moja** (1,000).",
          "When you count people, small numbers take the people prefix: **watoto wawili** (two children), **kumi na wawili** (twelve)."
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "swahili-time",
        "title": "Swahili time starts at sunrise",
        "body": [
          "Near the equator the sun rises around six o’clock every day of the year, and Swahili time counts from then. **Saa moja asubuhi**, “the first hour of the morning”, is 7 a.m.; **saa sita mchana** is noon; **saa moja jioni** is 7 p.m.",
          "To convert, add or take away six: Swahili **saa mbili** is 8 o’clock, **saa tatu** is 9, **saa nne** is 10. Always check whether someone means Swahili time or clock time!"
        ],
        "table": [
          ["Swahili time", "Clock time"],
          ["saa moja asubuhi", "7:00 a.m."],
          ["saa tatu asubuhi", "9:00 a.m."],
          ["saa sita mchana", "12:00 noon"],
          ["saa kumi jioni", "4:00 p.m."],
          ["saa mbili usiku", "8:00 p.m."]
        ],
        "targetColumns": [0]
      }
    ],
    "culture": [
      {
        "title": "Pole pole",
        "body": [
          "**Pole pole** (slowly) is advice you’ll hear on Kilimanjaro and in daily life alike. Things happen at their own pace, and greeting properly matters more than rushing."
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
